How much time does a data scientist typically spend on data wrangling? What are some of the reasons for this?
	Wes McKinney (2022) explicitly states that the industry standard is approximately 80% of a data scientist’s time is dedicated to the time on data wrangling, leaving only 20% for actual analysis and modeling. This highlights the fact that this is truly the bottleneck of a data scientist within the analytical pipeline.
	What I find to be interesting is that when I read this as being the standard, I immediately assumed that this is annoying or the “not fun” part of the job. However, McKinney frames these skills as the foundation of effective data science, arguing that without the ability to manipulate raw data into a structured form, advanced modeling is impossible. This couldn’t be more true and made me rethink the whole wrangling process as something to be more proud of when conducting. But why is this the case and not less considering that things become more efficient overtime?
	The reasons are multifaceted and deeply embedded in the nature of real-world data. If you think about it and have worked with data as an analyst, you are consistently met with a variety of inconsistencies and derived from various sources. One extremely relevant topic is that my job is in the process of acquiring another company. In these events, you can imagine that two disparate teams need to at some point converge logic together and “force” our datasets together. Some quick examples of this could be how we enter city and state information into the system. Does one system abbreviate while the other fully spells out the state name?
	When it comes to data sources, perhaps one system is exclusively utilizing an on premise SQL databases while the other utilizes Python for ETL and stores it within a cloud infrastructure that is NoSQL (schema-less structure like key value paired data). With both of these quick examples, you can imagine that although 80% seems alarming, it doesn’t seem far fetched considering the obstacles that I have and haven’t touched on. But remember, although it appears to be mundane, it is what separates you from a good to a great data scientist.
APA Citation
McKinney, W. (2022). Python for Data Analysis: Data Wrangling with Pandas, NumPy, and Jupyter (3rd ed.). O’Reilly Media.
